ct of this  a tremendous effort that he made there  went on the ship home Sam Rosenman who  was this top speechwriter and inside  Oval Office aide he spent the entire  voyage home trying to get Rozsival to  talk about the speech that he was going  to give to Congress about Yalta and  Rozsival just sat there like a zombies  staring into space for the entire voyage  you have a photograph the last one that  was taken of him that's the last picture  that was taken of Roosevelt yes down in  Warm Springs it was taken the day he  died and that is a very heartbreaking  picture to look at old is here he was 63  I believe I'm not too good on the you  but you paint a picture in your book  that he didn't like get along with  Winston Churchill he he he did not get  along with him as well as the war  progress they started out as best of  friends but really Roosevelt was very  hostile to the idea of continuing the  British Empire and when he got to the  summit conferences with Churchill and  Stalin he shifted the balance of his  attention completely to Stalin and left  Churchill as definitely a a weak third  party in the whole discussion and  Churchill really never forgave him for  that where do you find this kind of  information this is in many many records  of these particularly one of the best  sources I had was a Charles Bolin's  memoirs he was one of the into one of  the Russian experts at the State  Department and was the what was  Roosevelt's interpreter at Tehran and at  Yalta and his memoirs are extremely  penetrating and very critical of  Rozsival how long did you work on this  book I worked on it off and on since  1986 when I started working on that  article the big  leek which I wrote for American heritage  as a was published at American heritage  and off and on over the next 15 years I  would go back to it as I found more  information or more insights into it and  then for about two years before it went  to press I worked on a full time when  you write where do you write I write in  my own apartment in New York or up on a  little attic room in my Connecticut  house with an air-conditioner buzzing  away and I usually work about four to  six hours a day  I'm do you start early I regard it as a  job you get up at 8 o'clock and you  should be at work by a quarter to 9:00  there's no doubt about it and writing  for you is on a scale of 1 to 10 is it  10 being easy 1 being very hard it  varies from book to book a great deal  some books I have I wrote 1776 year of  illusions of also a sort of expose of  what was going on in that crucial year  that took me seven years to write and  that was agony I had so many so much  heavy research and so much revisions  this book was not in the final push  because they had accumulated so much  material this book was not such a hard  book to write I I felt this was a book  another thing that is always important  to me because I'm I guess of something  of a mystic like alright as a I felt  this was a book I was born to write this  was this the seeing that epic that that  phrase the New Deal is war so early in  my writing career and spoken by the man  who was my might say my literary mentor  it just gave me a feeling of momentum  that that drove me through this book and  made it I think of a very strong  narrative so what do you think so far  from what you've seen the reaction to  the book I think that people are  on the whole startled by it I don't  think that the complete reaction is  sinking in as yet III think that this is  a book that's going to have to be  absorbed by people who won't then talk  about it to each other and but I think  in the long run it's it's going to be  accepted as a as I say a first step  towards looking beyond the memories of  World War two into the history of reason  your biggest critic so far hmm a  reviewer in The Wall Street Journal  didn't like the book at all he he  thought that I was simply anti Roosevelt  and he really I would rather upset by  that review he distorted